Z||000000||fierospeeder||03-02-2001||12:12 AM||||Anyone that has a jc whitney catalog from the 70s would know what these are.<P>They are sparkplugs that only had the electrodes sticking out.  <P>I dont know much about these and i wondered if anyone had experience with them and what is the history about the plugs.<P>I know they were made in england and were suppose to provide more HP and make the car idle higher because they were so great.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||03-02-2001||09:38 AM||||I remember seeing the ads. They were "surface gap" spark plugs. There was a ring of 8-10 ground electrodes all the way around the center electrode. The center and ground electrodes were just about flush, I think. <BR>Seems like I remember hearing that Mercury Marine used a similar design in their outboard motors. Not sure about that, though.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000002||alpha||03-02-2001||10:18 AM||||I have a Mercury which uses surface gap plugs. They are not uncommon on other 2 strokes though...I think they may be less prone to fouling.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://fiero.dhs.org/fiero/sig.jpg">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu">douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierosound||03-02-2001||07:01 PM||||I've installed a set of TorqueMasters in my 3.4L and have had no problems, misfires etc. with these, AND, they're supposed to last the life of the engine!!<BR> <A HREF="http://www.torquemaster.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.torquemaster.com/</A> ||||reg||10
